1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,960
Look, we have only covered some of the basics here today.

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:11,160
I want to point out where you can find some more information to continue your agent journey.

3
00:00:11,160 --> 00:00:13,040
We've covered a lot of info.

4
00:00:13,040 --> 00:00:19,039
Here are some of my favorite resources for diving deeper into all that we just talked about.

5
00:00:19,039 --> 00:00:22,920
The Langtrain documentation is a great place to start.

6
00:00:22,920 --> 00:00:28,039
Specifically, it provides a great high-level overview of all the packages and services

7
00:00:28,039 --> 00:00:29,960
in the Langtrain ecosystem.

8
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:35,399
So we break down the different Langtrain core packages as well as all the Langtrain community

9
00:00:35,399 --> 00:00:36,399
packages.

10
00:00:36,399 --> 00:00:41,160
So we use Tavilia as part of Langtrain community and then we also use Langtrain OpenAI, which

11
00:00:41,160 --> 00:00:43,079
is a separate partner package.

12
00:00:43,079 --> 00:00:47,480
But we have a ton of other integrations and you can explore those here.

13
00:00:47,480 --> 00:00:51,799
Langtrain itself is more high-level and covers agents and other chains and different retrieval

14
00:00:51,799 --> 00:00:56,760
strategies that are good high-level entry points if you just want to get started.

15
00:00:56,759 --> 00:01:01,280
We also have a bunch of templates that can be deployed easily with Langserve.

16
00:01:01,280 --> 00:01:06,920
Langserve is just a really easy way to turn your Langtrain application into a web server.

17
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:09,000
Helping out with all of this is Langsmith.

18
00:01:09,000 --> 00:01:14,039
From the beginning, Langsmith can help with debugging and then it can also help with monitoring

19
00:01:14,039 --> 00:01:17,719
and production and it also has a playground, which we saw earlier.

20
00:01:17,719 --> 00:01:22,319
Looking at a few other sites, the Langtrain GitHub repo has a lot of good resources, a lot

21
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:26,800
of good cookbooks and a lot of templates for getting started.

22
00:01:26,800 --> 00:01:32,160
The Langgraph repo, of course, has in-depth documentation on Langgraph, everything that

23
00:01:32,160 --> 00:01:38,520
we just covered, so great reference docs, great tutorials and great Pouch2 guides.

24
00:01:38,520 --> 00:01:43,320
We've also done previous courses on Langtrain with deep learning that I'd highly recommend

25
00:01:43,320 --> 00:01:49,200
checking out in particular functions, tools, and agents with Langtrain is a really good precursor

26
00:01:49,200 --> 00:01:51,720
to this lesson.

27
00:01:51,719 --> 00:01:56,840
We already saw the prompt hub once, but just to reiterate, this is a great place to go

28
00:01:56,840 --> 00:02:00,319
to get inspiration and see what other expert prompters are doing.
